body:
Ares Management raised USD 31 billion in the third quarter of 2026, supported by fundraising across its private credit, real estate and infrastructure businesses. The Los Angeles-based alternative asset manager is on track for a record fundraising year as institutional investors continue to allocate capital to alternative investments.&#160;

More than half of the capital inflows during the quarter came from businesses outside its credit division. Ares' real assets division raised USD 11.2 billion, while its credit group attracted USD 15.2 billion. The company has expanded its presence beyond private credit through its acquisition of GCP International last year, strengthening its position in real estate and digital infrastructure.&#160;

Ares expects fundraising to remain strong in the fourth quarter, supported by demand from institutional investors and wealth management channels. Chief Executive Officer Michael Arougheti said the company continued to expect a record year for fundraising, with visibility into a strong final quarter.&#160;

The company's fundraising activities have included a Japan-focused logistics real estate development fund worth approximately USD 4 billion and its first secondaries fund, which raised USD 4.2 billion.&#160;

Ares' assets under management increased 16% to USD 690.6 billion in the three months ended September 30, 2026. Fee-related earnings rose 11% to USD 524.2 million during the quarter.&#160;

Investors were also watching management's comments on the artificial intelligence infrastructure boom, particularly after a selloff in AI stocks earlier in October raised questions about asset managers financing the expansion of AI infrastructure.&#160;

Despite strong fundraising, deal activity among private credit firms remained subdued during the quarter. Lower buyout activity and competition from the broadly syndicated loan market continued to affect investment activity.&#160;

Ares deployed USD 34.8 billion in capital during the quarter, driven by direct lending in the United States and Europe, alternative credit strategies, and infrastructure debt and equity investments. The company expects investment activity to improve in the fourth quarter.&#160;

For institutional funds, Ares generally begins earning management fees as capital is invested. Its uninvested capital increased 16% to USD 173.8 billion as of September 30, reflecting the amount available for future deployment. Arougheti said recent market volatility had increased demand for the firm's capital as it entered what is typically its strongest seasonal quarter.&#160;

Ares reported after-tax realised income per share of Class A common stock of USD 1.26 for the quarter, compared with USD 1.19 in the corresponding period a year earlier.&#160;

Ares' latest results highlight the role of real estate and infrastructure investments in broadening its fundraising base beyond private credit. However, the gap between capital raised and capital deployed remains important, as uninvested funds need to be put to work before they can generate management fees under the company's institutional fund structures. The fourth quarter will therefore be important for assessing whether investment activity improves as expected. Fundraising demand, deal availability and competition in lending markets will remain key factors shaping the company's performance.&#160;
